WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.010 --> 00:00:09.000
For those of you who don't know
me, my name is Meghan Harris.

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:11.650
I am a data scientist at
Memorial Sloan Kettering Cancer

00:00:11.650 --> 00:00:12.520
Center.

00:00:12.520 --> 00:00:14.500
And I'm here today
because I have a story

00:00:14.500 --> 00:00:17.290
to tell you all about
data pipelines and R.

00:00:17.290 --> 00:00:20.260
But before I get into that,
I do have a quick question

00:00:20.260 --> 00:00:21.120
for all of you.

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:22.960
You don't have to
answer me out loud,

00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:26.290
but when you see
this image, does it

00:00:26.290 --> 00:00:30.820
make you feel anything or
have any ideas on what it is?

00:00:30.820 --> 00:00:35.590
Well, this is my personal
100% accurate representation

00:00:35.590 --> 00:00:38.440
of a data pipeline,
and now you're

00:00:38.440 --> 00:00:40.750
all thinking I'm out
of my mind, and I

00:00:40.750 --> 00:00:42.850
might be, but hear me out.

00:00:42.850 --> 00:00:46.330
Of course, this isn't an
actual data pipeline, right?

00:00:46.330 --> 00:00:48.640
But rather the mental
images and feelings

00:00:48.640 --> 00:00:51.280
I had when I was tasked
with making a data

00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:55.270
pipeline from scratch in my last
position as a data specialist.

00:00:55.270 --> 00:00:56.980
The story I want to
tell you all today

00:00:56.980 --> 00:00:58.990
is about how I
created that pipeline

00:00:58.990 --> 00:01:01.690
in R within the
context of me being

00:01:01.690 --> 00:01:03.910
a self-taught, programmer.

00:01:03.910 --> 00:01:05.590
Those of us that
work in data, when

00:01:05.590 --> 00:01:09.190
we think of data pipelines,
I sure as hell hope it

00:01:09.190 --> 00:01:10.720
does not look like that.

00:01:10.720 --> 00:01:13.820
But hopefully it looks maybe
something more like this.

00:01:13.820 --> 00:01:17.860
A perfect data pipeline, clean
processes, clean structures,

00:01:17.860 --> 00:01:19.000
great results.

00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:22.960
This is a pipeline to most of
us, but in the last position

00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:26.740
I was in, the pipeline looked
nothing like that at all.

00:01:26.740 --> 00:01:30.350
If we could even say there was
a pipeline, it was very clunky.

00:01:30.350 --> 00:01:33.730
No structure, no
automation, just bad vibes

00:01:33.730 --> 00:01:35.200
and recklessness.

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:40.000
So, although I have learned a
lot and I have so much to share,

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:41.770
I don't have a lot
of time to talk.

00:01:41.770 --> 00:01:43.850
Y'all we'll be lucky if
we get under 19 minutes,

00:01:43.850 --> 00:01:44.980
I'm going to be honest.

00:01:44.980 --> 00:01:47.650
So I pulled out four
main points that I

00:01:47.650 --> 00:01:51.040
learned about creating
pipelines during this journey.

00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:53.230
These are going to touch
on my first encounters

00:01:53.230 --> 00:01:55.570
within the data and the
pipeline, the importance

00:01:55.570 --> 00:01:58.600
of identifying the environmental
structures of the pipeline,

00:01:58.600 --> 00:02:01.300
embedding validations
within the pipeline,

00:02:01.300 --> 00:02:03.880
and understanding what
sustainability looks like

00:02:03.880 --> 00:02:05.110
for the pipeline.

00:02:05.110 --> 00:02:07.450
Now keep in mind that
all of these main points

00:02:07.450 --> 00:02:09.850
could easily be their own
talks, their own lectures,

00:02:09.850 --> 00:02:10.910
their own courses.

00:02:10.910 --> 00:02:13.580
So I'll only touch on
the biggest takeaways.

00:02:13.580 --> 00:02:17.260
However, I have created a list
of example pipeline scripts,

00:02:17.260 --> 00:02:20.830
actual Shell template
documents that you guys can all

00:02:20.830 --> 00:02:24.790
use that is in the repo
for this talk on GitHub.

00:02:24.790 --> 00:02:25.680
Yes, I know.

00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:27.220
Great right.

00:02:27.220 --> 00:02:29.380
So with that out
of the way, I think

00:02:29.380 --> 00:02:31.870
the best thing for me to do
is go back to the beginning

00:02:31.870 --> 00:02:34.760
during those first encounters
I had with this pipeline.

00:02:34.760 --> 00:02:37.270
And guys I know I'm
going to sound really

00:02:37.270 --> 00:02:39.370
out of my mind again,
but the very first thing

00:02:39.370 --> 00:02:44.450
I learned about making data
pipelines in R was to not open R. 

00:02:44.450 --> 00:02:48.460
Ah, now I know you're thinking
Megan we're at RStudio conf, what

00:02:48.460 --> 00:02:49.540
are you talking about?

00:02:49.540 --> 00:02:50.930
I know, but hear me out.

00:02:50.930 --> 00:02:52.690
Let me give you some context.

00:02:52.690 --> 00:02:54.640
This was me on my
first day as a data

00:02:54.640 --> 00:02:56.930
specialist, my first
professional data position.

00:02:56.930 --> 00:02:59.390
I am self-taught so
I'm already coming in.

00:02:59.390 --> 00:03:01.540
Not familiar with
things, but I'm

00:03:01.540 --> 00:03:03.620
also not familiar with
the subject matter.

00:03:03.620 --> 00:03:06.310
This physician was
dealing with opioid data.

00:03:06.310 --> 00:03:08.680
There was a lot of data
existing everywhere

00:03:08.680 --> 00:03:11.380
and the organization I
was working directly with

00:03:11.380 --> 00:03:13.690
did not have any
structure in place,

00:03:13.690 --> 00:03:15.700
nor did they have
any expectations

00:03:15.700 --> 00:03:17.890
about what I was to do.

00:03:17.890 --> 00:03:21.220
So if I am a new self-taught
programmer newbie

00:03:21.220 --> 00:03:24.930
and I'm coming in with no
direction, what did I do?

00:03:24.930 --> 00:03:27.300
So just like this
dude is jumping head

00:03:27.300 --> 00:03:30.790
first to get to the Winchester,
shout out to Shaun of the Dead.

00:03:30.790 --> 00:03:34.200
I also dove in
headfirst into RStudio

00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:37.000
as soon as I got any data,
and that's what I did.

00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:39.120
Whenever I got a new
data set, I tried

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:41.640
to create R
programs immediately

00:03:41.640 --> 00:03:45.130
and what I should have done was
actually started investigating.

00:03:45.130 --> 00:03:47.110
I didn't ask basic questions.

00:03:47.110 --> 00:03:48.930
I didn't ask for
code books, I didn't

00:03:48.930 --> 00:03:50.940
try to validate anything.

00:03:50.940 --> 00:03:53.440
It was just recklessness
and vibes, I told you guys.

00:03:53.440 --> 00:03:57.480
But I argue this point
of not opening RStudio

00:03:57.480 --> 00:03:59.610
with an example of
something that happens

00:03:59.610 --> 00:04:01.320
to me that was traumatic.

00:04:01.320 --> 00:04:03.270
I call it the Narcan story.

00:04:03.270 --> 00:04:04.890
Now, for those of
you who don't know,

00:04:04.890 --> 00:04:06.750
Narcan is a medication
that is used

00:04:06.750 --> 00:04:08.913
to reverse opioid overdoses.

00:04:08.913 --> 00:04:10.830
So, of course, working
with this type of data,

00:04:10.830 --> 00:04:13.110
I'm going to see this
information at some point.

00:04:13.110 --> 00:04:14.970
One day I get a data
set and there is

00:04:14.970 --> 00:04:17.250
a variable in it called Narcan.

00:04:17.250 --> 00:04:20.760
And like I did, opened up
RStudio, quickly, looked at it

00:04:20.760 --> 00:04:24.790
and saw, oh, Narcan has
numbers in it, OK numbers.

00:04:24.790 --> 00:04:27.660
So without doing anything
else, I immediately

00:04:27.660 --> 00:04:30.460
start making automated
reports, dashboards.

00:04:30.460 --> 00:04:33.460
I'm feeling like I am a
freakin' superwoman, man.

00:04:33.460 --> 00:04:34.590
I'm awesome.

00:04:34.590 --> 00:04:37.410
And, you know, I just I woke
up in the middle of the night,

00:04:37.410 --> 00:04:40.440
like, months after that,
and I had just a random

00:04:40.440 --> 00:04:43.570
just Eureka moment, kind of
felt like something was wrong.

00:04:43.570 --> 00:04:46.540
I woke up and I was like, HMM,
something ain't right, chief.

00:04:46.540 --> 00:04:47.190
What is it?

00:04:47.190 --> 00:04:52.740
So I went to RStudio, I
opened it up and I saw a value

00:04:52.740 --> 00:04:54.880
and it was 4 plus.

00:04:54.880 --> 00:05:00.330
And guys, my heart sunk
because 4 plus is not a number.

00:05:00.330 --> 00:05:02.100
4 plus is a string.

00:05:02.100 --> 00:05:05.730
So four months I was
calculating off of this variable

00:05:05.730 --> 00:05:07.620
that I did not even
know how, but I

00:05:07.620 --> 00:05:09.510
was coercing a
character variable

00:05:09.510 --> 00:05:11.140
into an numeric variable.

00:05:11.140 --> 00:05:13.440
So imagine the
embarrassment of going back

00:05:13.440 --> 00:05:15.180
after everyone's
relying on me for this

00:05:15.180 --> 00:05:19.110
and being like, hey
guys, it's wrong.

00:05:19.110 --> 00:05:23.090
So that was just one example of
many times that happened to me.

00:05:23.090 --> 00:05:23.590
All right.

00:05:23.590 --> 00:05:26.190
And clearly, you can see
here, I learned a lot,

00:05:26.190 --> 00:05:30.000
but the main thing I learned
was to not jump into RStudio

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:31.660
as soon as you get a data set.

00:05:31.660 --> 00:05:33.840
If you do want to
jump in it, only

00:05:33.840 --> 00:05:36.540
use it to give you a
little bit of context,

00:05:36.540 --> 00:05:40.050
a little bit of clues so that
you can ask more information.

00:05:40.050 --> 00:05:41.580
It was a really
hard lesson that I

00:05:41.580 --> 00:05:44.190
had to learn, to just go
through this iterative process

00:05:44.190 --> 00:05:46.590
of asking questions,
but doing so

00:05:46.590 --> 00:05:49.320
gave me just a little
bit more clarity.

00:05:49.320 --> 00:05:52.020
So remember how that pipeline
looked like in my head

00:05:52.020 --> 00:05:54.540
when I dove in headfirst
with no information?

00:05:54.540 --> 00:05:56.490
Well, now that I had
an investigation,

00:05:56.490 --> 00:05:59.010
it looks still messy.

00:05:59.010 --> 00:06:01.480
It looks messy, and it
doesn't seem like much change.

00:06:01.480 --> 00:06:04.140
But I guarantee you this
is a different picture.

00:06:04.140 --> 00:06:06.190
But just enough
started to clear up.

00:06:06.190 --> 00:06:08.970
So that I could accept
that fact that I needed

00:06:08.970 --> 00:06:10.570
to start building on this.

00:06:10.570 --> 00:06:12.840
I needed to make some structure.

00:06:12.840 --> 00:06:15.120
So at this point,
we're going to try

00:06:15.120 --> 00:06:16.960
to add some environmental
structure, right?

00:06:16.960 --> 00:06:19.502
So the biggest thing I learned
about environmental structures

00:06:19.502 --> 00:06:21.510
is that theta
pipelines can actually

00:06:21.510 --> 00:06:24.240
exist in two types of
environments, something

00:06:24.240 --> 00:06:27.640
that I'm terming as external
and internal environments.

00:06:27.640 --> 00:06:29.970
Now, there's probably way
more sophisticated ways

00:06:29.970 --> 00:06:31.750
to think about this
and break this down.

00:06:31.750 --> 00:06:34.920
But I'm a beginner and
this is what my beginner

00:06:34.920 --> 00:06:36.550
brain like made sense.

00:06:36.550 --> 00:06:38.160
This is what made sense.

00:06:38.160 --> 00:06:40.380
So when starting
out, I had realized

00:06:40.380 --> 00:06:43.110
they had external things
outside of my computer

00:06:43.110 --> 00:06:45.660
that was affecting the
data inside of my computer,

00:06:45.660 --> 00:06:49.410
but also things inside of my
computer, inside of RStudio

00:06:49.410 --> 00:06:51.510
that was affecting it as well.

00:06:51.510 --> 00:06:53.070
And I'll explain both of them.

00:06:53.070 --> 00:06:55.050
So when I say
external environment,

00:06:55.050 --> 00:06:58.270
I'm talking about more
abstract concepts.

00:06:58.270 --> 00:07:01.830
I'm talking about any kind of
data, interactions, decisions,

00:07:01.830 --> 00:07:05.560
admin policies that can
affect the flow of the data.

00:07:05.560 --> 00:07:08.580
Even the technology that's used
for data collection and even

00:07:08.580 --> 00:07:11.340
the data literacy of whoever
you are working with.

00:07:11.340 --> 00:07:14.340
It was so important to
identify all of these concepts

00:07:14.340 --> 00:07:17.250
because at some
point, they are going

00:07:17.250 --> 00:07:19.930
to affect your internal
flow of your pipeline.

00:07:19.930 --> 00:07:23.400
It's going to affect how you end
up working with and structuring

00:07:23.400 --> 00:07:25.110
your data internally.

00:07:25.110 --> 00:07:26.790
Now, the internal
environment is what

00:07:26.790 --> 00:07:29.220
most people think about when
they hear the words data

00:07:29.220 --> 00:07:29.860
pipeline.

00:07:29.860 --> 00:07:31.830
They think about the
actual file structures,

00:07:31.830 --> 00:07:33.870
the flow of things
happening internally,

00:07:33.870 --> 00:07:36.150
the logic of processing,
and even the data

00:07:36.150 --> 00:07:37.960
security and storage methods.

00:07:37.960 --> 00:07:40.350
Now, it should go
without saying that this

00:07:40.350 --> 00:07:41.680
isn't the reality, right?

00:07:41.680 --> 00:07:44.400
We don't have two
separate environments

00:07:44.400 --> 00:07:46.060
that exist in silos.

00:07:46.060 --> 00:07:48.720
There's external things that
affect internal things and vice

00:07:48.720 --> 00:07:49.420
versa.

00:07:49.420 --> 00:07:51.390
And this is a challenge
that most every data

00:07:51.390 --> 00:07:52.570
person knows well.

00:07:52.570 --> 00:07:55.500
Even if we don't think about
environments in this way,

00:07:55.500 --> 00:07:59.940
I'm sure that if any of you
work in nonprofit or academia

00:07:59.940 --> 00:08:02.490
or government or
anywhere, you probably

00:08:02.490 --> 00:08:06.420
have some form of bureaucracy
affecting what you do,

00:08:06.420 --> 00:08:09.070
maybe even conflicting
interests in decisions.

00:08:09.070 --> 00:08:10.750
That's an example of this.

00:08:10.750 --> 00:08:12.900
So what helps me
to better navigate

00:08:12.900 --> 00:08:15.300
the interactions between
these two environments

00:08:15.300 --> 00:08:17.580
was to repeatedly
tackle each environment

00:08:17.580 --> 00:08:20.970
by introducing some structure,
some documentation for one,

00:08:20.970 --> 00:08:22.890
and adjusting for the other.

00:08:22.890 --> 00:08:24.640
So I just want to
give a really quick.

00:08:24.640 --> 00:08:26.420
A sample of just
some documents that I

00:08:26.420 --> 00:08:28.280
made to try to help
me out with this.

00:08:28.280 --> 00:08:30.170
So when I was thinking
about structuring

00:08:30.170 --> 00:08:32.240
my external
environment, you guys

00:08:32.240 --> 00:08:34.830
got to remember I was
a one person data team.

00:08:34.830 --> 00:08:37.429
Really, I was the only
person coding an R,

00:08:37.429 --> 00:08:39.740
so everyone else that
I was working with

00:08:39.740 --> 00:08:43.289
had no concept of this, did
not do any coding, whatever.

00:08:43.289 --> 00:08:46.550
So my method of trying to
introduce some structure

00:08:46.550 --> 00:08:48.840
was to get everybody
on the same page.

00:08:48.840 --> 00:08:52.430
So for this example, I have
up here making metadata

00:08:52.430 --> 00:08:56.240
and very loosely metadata is
literally just data about data.

00:08:56.240 --> 00:09:01.310
What helped everybody else
around me to kind of help me

00:09:01.310 --> 00:09:03.290
with what they wanted
was to make sure

00:09:03.290 --> 00:09:05.100
that they understood
what was happening.

00:09:05.100 --> 00:09:08.550
So what was the name of
the file like for the data?

00:09:08.550 --> 00:09:09.900
How often was it collected?

00:09:09.900 --> 00:09:11.242
How was it collected?

00:09:11.242 --> 00:09:12.950
Those simple things
we don't think about,

00:09:12.950 --> 00:09:16.050
it really helps to have
it documents it somewhere.

00:09:16.050 --> 00:09:17.640
Another example, I
have is something

00:09:17.640 --> 00:09:20.440
that I'm terming as
assess, attempt, repeat.

00:09:20.440 --> 00:09:21.680
We all do this.

00:09:21.680 --> 00:09:24.157
How many of you have gone
to a new organisation?

00:09:24.157 --> 00:09:26.490
Saw the way that they were
doing something and was like,

00:09:26.490 --> 00:09:28.710
HMM, this ain't right.

00:09:28.710 --> 00:09:32.010
So you get in, you
assess your situation

00:09:32.010 --> 00:09:34.240
and you attempt
to make a change.

00:09:34.240 --> 00:09:38.010
Now, if we're lucky, it's
the attempt is successful

00:09:38.010 --> 00:09:38.860
and it's great.

00:09:38.860 --> 00:09:41.377
And what usually happens
is that we either

00:09:41.377 --> 00:09:42.960
have people who have
been there that's

00:09:42.960 --> 00:09:46.430
just like we're not doing that
or something just doesn't work.

00:09:46.430 --> 00:09:48.510
So when I tried to
do this, I would

00:09:48.510 --> 00:09:51.120
try to make the change outside
externally, maybe in a way

00:09:51.120 --> 00:09:53.050
that we collect
data or anything.

00:09:53.050 --> 00:09:55.110
But nine times out
of 10 I ended up

00:09:55.110 --> 00:09:58.020
having to compromise and
make up for it internally

00:09:58.020 --> 00:10:00.170
in my systems.

00:10:00.170 --> 00:10:01.850
So speaking of
internally, there's

00:10:01.850 --> 00:10:04.370
also some examples
of introducing

00:10:04.370 --> 00:10:06.140
some structure this way.

00:10:06.140 --> 00:10:08.330
So the first
example, I have here

00:10:08.330 --> 00:10:10.920
is simply named "make
clean file structures".

00:10:10.920 --> 00:10:12.710
And this is so much
more than like, oh,

00:10:12.710 --> 00:10:14.703
making sure your
folder names are clean

00:10:14.703 --> 00:10:16.620
and making sure your
data is in a data folder.

00:10:16.620 --> 00:10:18.120
Your scripts are
in a script folder.

00:10:18.120 --> 00:10:20.990
This also encompasses
knowing how

00:10:20.990 --> 00:10:22.930
to work with R project files.

00:10:22.930 --> 00:10:25.250
This encompasses
knowing where you

00:10:25.250 --> 00:10:28.010
are in your working directory,
knowing knowledge about working

00:10:28.010 --> 00:10:29.780
directories, because
that's the only way

00:10:29.780 --> 00:10:32.810
that you are going to get
a full flow automation

00:10:32.810 --> 00:10:35.390
process with everything
stored organized

00:10:35.390 --> 00:10:38.150
in a good way and seamless.

00:10:38.150 --> 00:10:42.110
And like I said, if you need to
more about working directories

00:10:42.110 --> 00:10:44.150
or R project
files or anything,

00:10:44.150 --> 00:10:47.030
Jenny Bryan and all
of the amazing people

00:10:47.030 --> 00:10:48.740
have already made
resources for it,

00:10:48.740 --> 00:10:51.870
especially with the here package
and that is linked on my repo.

00:10:51.870 --> 00:10:55.130
I'm not going to act like
I know about that too much.

00:10:55.130 --> 00:10:56.870
The next example,
I have is something

00:10:56.870 --> 00:10:59.220
that I'm terming as
modlarizing your code.

00:10:59.220 --> 00:11:01.280
Now, in this example,
it's being modularized

00:11:01.280 --> 00:11:03.140
by data and component.

00:11:03.140 --> 00:11:05.750
So this is a snip from
an actual large project

00:11:05.750 --> 00:11:08.940
I had where I had so
many data sources, right?

00:11:08.940 --> 00:11:12.890
So it made sense for me to do
the work for each data source

00:11:12.890 --> 00:11:14.720
within one script.

00:11:14.720 --> 00:11:16.530
Now, that's only
one way to do it.

00:11:16.530 --> 00:11:20.180
You know, that's it might
work for you and it might not,

00:11:20.180 --> 00:11:20.910
you know.

00:11:20.910 --> 00:11:23.240
But another example
that you can also do

00:11:23.240 --> 00:11:26.670
is modularizing your code
by function or by process.

00:11:26.670 --> 00:11:29.690
And I usually do this when
I don't have too many data

00:11:29.690 --> 00:11:32.450
sources and I have a
more manageable project.

00:11:32.450 --> 00:11:35.210
Now in this example, I'm
showing three scripts

00:11:35.210 --> 00:11:36.870
in my working directory here.

00:11:36.870 --> 00:11:38.390
I have a docking
script, which I'll

00:11:38.390 --> 00:11:39.920
explain that in the next slide.

00:11:39.920 --> 00:11:41.810
I have a processing
script which is just

00:11:41.810 --> 00:11:44.000
for doing transformations
and cleaning,

00:11:44.000 --> 00:11:46.250
and I have a
visualization script

00:11:46.250 --> 00:11:49.070
or this script for short,
which is literally just making

00:11:49.070 --> 00:11:50.540
vizzes, right?

00:11:50.540 --> 00:11:52.790
So what helped me
with these projects

00:11:52.790 --> 00:11:54.920
was having individual
scripts that I

00:11:54.920 --> 00:11:59.400
could call on when it was time
to use it in the pipeline.

00:11:59.400 --> 00:12:01.620
So the last example, I
have here for structuring

00:12:01.620 --> 00:12:03.270
internal environments
is something

00:12:03.270 --> 00:12:05.873
that I'm calling connecting and
chaining where it makes sense.

00:12:05.873 --> 00:12:08.290
And honestly, I could have
just called it docking scripts.

00:12:08.290 --> 00:12:09.665
I don't know why
I talk too much.

00:12:09.665 --> 00:12:12.090
So an example of
a docking script

00:12:12.090 --> 00:12:13.860
is on these next
few slides, and I

00:12:13.860 --> 00:12:16.290
want to stress the
importance of the content

00:12:16.290 --> 00:12:19.230
in it is not so much important,
but just the logic of what

00:12:19.230 --> 00:12:23.940
is happening is. Now, in my
terms in my Megan brain,

00:12:23.940 --> 00:12:27.390
a docking script is one R
script that will literally

00:12:27.390 --> 00:12:30.480
bring in your data, do your
cleaning, your validation,

00:12:30.480 --> 00:12:32.230
do everything that
it needs to do.

00:12:32.230 --> 00:12:34.680
So that when you hit that
source button at the top,

00:12:34.680 --> 00:12:36.580
everything does
what it needs to do.

00:12:36.580 --> 00:12:38.790
And your end result is
hopefully a deliverable

00:12:38.790 --> 00:12:41.310
that you're looking for,
whether it is a Shiny

00:12:41.310 --> 00:12:43.650
app or a clean data set.

00:12:43.650 --> 00:12:46.170
So just to get into
it just a little bit,

00:12:46.170 --> 00:12:48.880
the docking script starts
just like any other R script

00:12:48.880 --> 00:12:51.060
will start. We'll
load in our libraries,

00:12:51.060 --> 00:12:52.800
then the next
section will go into

00:12:52.800 --> 00:12:54.730
is something that I
call the data process

00:12:54.730 --> 00:12:55.450
check here.

00:12:55.450 --> 00:12:57.960
Now this looks maybe
hopefully not complicated,

00:12:57.960 --> 00:13:00.330
but this is literally
just bringing in your data

00:13:00.330 --> 00:13:04.120
and then determining how you
want to have some logic values.

00:13:04.120 --> 00:13:07.140
So if you want to do something
to check like very simply down

00:13:07.140 --> 00:13:10.260
here, this last line, it's
just checking a new data

00:13:10.260 --> 00:13:13.560
set against an old data set
to see if the value of rows

00:13:13.560 --> 00:13:14.110
change.

00:13:14.110 --> 00:13:15.700
And I don't
recommend doing that.

00:13:15.700 --> 00:13:18.180
I don't have time to get
into why, but don't do it.

00:13:18.180 --> 00:13:23.190
But you'll get a logic value
at the end of this section.

00:13:23.190 --> 00:13:24.930
So then the next
section I have here

00:13:24.930 --> 00:13:26.710
is a logic flow for processing.

00:13:26.710 --> 00:13:28.270
So this is just very simple.

00:13:28.270 --> 00:13:30.130
If scripts, if statements.

00:13:30.130 --> 00:13:34.930
So if the data has updated,
we will then tell R to source

00:13:34.930 --> 00:13:36.700
one of our modularized codes.

00:13:36.700 --> 00:13:38.970
We will tell R that OK,
if there's new data,

00:13:38.970 --> 00:13:40.960
go ahead and process that data.

00:13:40.960 --> 00:13:43.170
If there's new data,
go ahead and make

00:13:43.170 --> 00:13:44.700
a visualization for it.

00:13:44.700 --> 00:13:46.920
So that can look very
complicated, obviously,

00:13:46.920 --> 00:13:49.500
depending on what you got
going on in your project.

00:13:49.500 --> 00:13:51.900
But after all of
that is done, you

00:13:51.900 --> 00:13:54.930
can embed options in
your docking script

00:13:54.930 --> 00:13:56.890
to render your output.

00:13:56.890 --> 00:14:01.530
So in this case, it's a simple
HTML report and an example

00:14:01.530 --> 00:14:04.950
of a working, hopefully working,
version of this is on the repo,

00:14:04.950 --> 00:14:05.970
you can see.

00:14:05.970 --> 00:14:09.780
So now that I've zoomed by that
and realize that, oh, I needed

00:14:09.780 --> 00:14:12.210
